        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.782,
            0.104,
            0.9,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "MATHEMATICS"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.139,
            0.713,
            0.159
          ],
          "content": "**So, Aftab and his daughter are 42 and 12 years old, respectively.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.146,
            0.166,
            0.901,
            0.186
          ],
          "content": "**Verify this answer by checking if it satisfies the conditions of the given problems.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.199,
            0.902,
            0.239
          ],
          "content": "**Example 6 : In a shop the cost of 2 pencils and 3 erasers is** $^ { \\gtrless 9 }$ **and the cost of 4 pencils and 6 erasers is** \\`**18. Find the cost of each pencil and each eraser.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.246,
            0.605,
            0.266
          ],
          "content": "**Solution : The pair of linear equations formed were:**"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.391,
            0.272,
            0.898,
            0.291
          ],
          "content": "2x + 3y = 9\\tag{1}",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.391,
            0.297,
            0.898,
            0.316
          ],
          "content": "4x + 6y = 18\\tag{2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.322,
            0.876,
            0.342
          ],
          "content": "**We first express the value of** x **in terms of** y **from the equation** $2 x + 3 y = 9$ **, to get**"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.45,
            0.351,
            0.898,
            0.393
          ],
          "content": "x =   { \\frac { 9 - 3 y } { 2 } }\\tag{3}",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.402,
            0.633,
            0.422
          ],
          "content": "**Now we substitute this value of** x **in Equation (2), to get**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.482,
            0.14,
            0.501
          ],
          "content": "**i.e.,**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.507,
            0.141,
            0.526
          ],
          "content": "**i.e.,**"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.315,
            0.433,
            0.53,
            0.526
          ],
          "content": "\\left\\{ \\begin{aligned} 4(9 - 3y) \\\\ 2 + 6y = 18 \\\\ 18 - 6y + 6y = 18 \\\\ 0 \\quad 18 = 18 \\end{aligned} \\right.",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.544,
            0.903,
            0.67
          ],
          "content": "**This statement is true for all values of** $y .$ **However, we do not get a specific value of** y **as a solution. Therefore, we cannot obtain a specific value of** x**. This situation has arisen because both the given equations are the same. Therefore, Equations (1) and (2) have** infinitely many solutions. **We cannot find a unique cost of a pencil and an eraser, because there are many common solutions, to the given situation.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.68,
            0.642,
            0.699
          ],
          "content": "**Example 7 : Two rails are represented by the equations**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.702,
            0.753,
            0.721
          ],
          "content": "$x + 2y - 4 = 0$ **and** $2x + 4y - 12 = 0$ **. Will the rails cross each other?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.725,
            0.605,
            0.744
          ],
          "content": "**Solution : The pair of linear equations formed were:**"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.343,
            0.748,
            0.898,
            0.788
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{aligned}x + 2y - 4 &= \\left. 0 \\right. \\\\2x + 4y - 12 &= \\left. 0 \\right.\\end{aligned}\\tag{1}",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_18.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.868,
            0.771,
            0.898,
            0.787
          ],
          "content": "**(2)**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.792,
            0.596,
            0.812
          ],
          "content": "**We express** x **in terms of** y **from Equation (1) to get**"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.966,
            0.564,
            0.983
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.45,
            0.814,
            0.561,
            0.832
          ],
          "content": "x = 4 - 2 y",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_22.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.836,
            0.632,
            0.855
          ],
          "content": "**Now, we substitute this value of** x **in Equation (2) to get**"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.278,
            0.858,
            0.515,
            0.877
          ],
          "content": "2(4 - 2y) + 4y - 12 = 0",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_24.jpg"
        }
      ]
    }
